WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.709 --> 00:00:07.120
Bringing back dinosaurs would probably end
in an… unfortunate outcome, as the Jurassic

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.790
Park franchise has shown us countless times.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:14.870
So, I think most of us would agree that while
resurrecting dinosaurs through some miraculous

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.401
future technology would be cool, it’s almost
certainly not a good idea.

00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:23.769
But I can’t deny, I’ve still always been
interested in the concept of a responsibly-run

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:28.509
prehistoric reserve where nothing goes wrong
and the dinosaurs don’t eat you.

00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:33.630
That’s the basic concept behind the fictional
park of Prehistorica, a biological nature

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:38.440
preserve where resurrected dinosaurs and other
prehistoric animals are treated with care

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.940
and the fences actually work.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:45.850
Prehistorica is a worldbuilding project created
by Joshua Dunlop, a talented artist and creator

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.879
who you can follow and support in the links
below.

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:53.710
And since we sadly can’t truly visit Prehistorica,
for this entry into the archive, I’ll be

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.420
taking on the role of tour guide and giving
you a glimpse of the well-managed prehistoric

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.159
reserve that I think we’ve all secretly
wanted to vacation at…

00:01:02.159 --> 00:01:06.680
The fictional reserve is set in the future,
on an enormous artificial island.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:12.180
Aptly named ‘Pangea,’ the island supports
over twenty different biomes including tropical

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.340
and subtropical rainforests, deserts, mountains,
tundra, woodlands, grasslands, mangroves,

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:22.460
and more — all of which are home to a variety
of prehistoric life.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:27.090
Our tour will take us across the entire island,
and we’ll learn quite a bit about the species

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.509
here along the way!

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:33.730
So, get ready to discover the wonders of a
world lost to time…

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:37.290
Welcome to Torosaurus Trail, the first stop
on our tour.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:42.110
Here, you can drive through the vast open
plains in an autonomous vehicle, and get a

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:45.460
close look at the one-and-only Torosaurus
herd.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.759
These large frilled herbivores have grown
accustomed to the armored viewing vehicles,

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:55.100
and will often lumber close by them — allowing
for some exciting close encounters.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.329
And next to these vehicles, it’s easier
to appreciate just how large even the newborns

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:00.329
can get.

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:04.890
While you observe these magnificent creatures,
a park ranger might point out the difference

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.740
between triceratops and torosaurus.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.270
Some scientists have actually theorized the
larger torosaurus is actually just the adult

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:17.670
form of triceratops — although more recent
studies indicate they’re a distinctive genus.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:22.390
If unique, Torosaurus had one of the largest
skulls of any known land animal!

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.100
Fascinating stuff.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:28.830
Away from the plains, in the misty jungles
of Majungasaurus Mountain, you’ll have the

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.490
opportunity to see some truly mighty theropods.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:37.430
From an underground viewing station, you can
get up close and personal with the only majungasaurus

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.360
pack on Prehistorica.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.410
Right now, it’s feeding time, and dinner
is delivered in from above by custom drones

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:49.690
that lower the specialized lab-grown meat
safely into the majungasaurus habitat — a

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.310
vast region, which, like most environments,
is sectioned off with advanced futuristic

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:59.340
barriers to ensure the dinosaurs don’t eat
each other… or parks guests.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.650
While you watch the majungasaurus pack chow
down on their air-delivered meal, you might

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:08.610
be interested to learn their teeth are serrated,
and they have more teeth than most genuses

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.790
in their family.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:14.870
These fearsome fangs likely helped majungasaurus
take down small sauropods like Rapetosaurus

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:20.350
— a theory supported by Majungasaurus toothmarks
on Rapetosaurus bones.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:25.630
Down the mountain, you can take a stroll through
the calming, temperate Wildwood, where you

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:28.080
might spot some gentle giants.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.320
This unique genus of stegosaurid herbivores
is incredibly well mannered, but can be a

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.640
little clumsy with the large point spikes
on their bodies which help them defend from

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:38.640
predators.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.160
So, it’s best to view the Kentrosaurus from
a safe distance on one of the many elevated

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.180
viewing platforms.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:49.330
If you’re wondering about the spikes, we
can theorize from real fossils that Kentrosaurus

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:54.520
had long, pointed spikes in the back transitioning
into shorter and flatter plates near the front

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.830
— with 15 pairs in total!

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:01.830
In short: Kentrosaurus was likely a very well-defended
dinosaur.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:05.580
If you’re not afraid of a little adventure,
you can jump on a guide boat and make your

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.320
way down the muddy Diabloceratops River.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.780
Here, you might be able to spot a Diabloceratops
wading through the slow-moving waters to cool

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.000
off in the hot afternoon sun.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.519
To ensure these animals aren’t pulled out
of their natural rhythms, the tour boats don’t

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:21.549
get too close.

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:27.409
It’s worth noting the concept of Diabloceratops
wading in rivers like a hippopotamus is purely

00:04:27.409 --> 00:04:28.409
speculative.

00:04:28.409 --> 00:04:29.409
Many of the most unique behaviors of modern
animals wouldn’t be preserved in the fossil

00:04:29.409 --> 00:04:30.409
record, so we can assume the same would be
true for dinosaurs.

00:04:30.409 --> 00:04:32.789
And since Diabloceratops likely lived in a
region full of lakes and floodplains during

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:37.090
the Cretaceous, it’s a logical guess that
a good wallowing would keep the horned-faced

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:38.860
herbivores comfortable.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:43.710
Further downriver, the tour boats drift around
the bend of Suchomimus Stream, a region where

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:48.340
you might see some of Prehistorica’s river-dwelling
carnivores.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:53.710
Believe it or not, the animal you see here
is just a juvenile, and the adults get considerably

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:54.710
larger.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.919
But don’t worry, the artist states these
water giants can’t get close to the boat

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:04.010
thanks to an invisible fence system and non-intrusive
underwater barriers that keep their toothy

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:06.870
jaws far away from park visitors.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:11.930
Some paleontologists believe Suchomimus is
a variant of the better known Baryonyx.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:17.219
If unique, Suchomimus was likely larger than
its cousin, with some estimates reaching 34

00:05:17.219 --> 00:05:19.150
feet, or 10.3 meters.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:24.720
And the author notes specimens of Baryonyx
can also be found on Prehistorica, just in

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.930
a separate region, as both species are quite
territorial.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:33.699
Venturing all the way out to the tropical
coast, there’s an opportunity to get a bird’s-eye-view

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:35.860
of the island of Pangea.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:41.030
If you take a ride in a Coastal Skybox, you’ll
get not only a view of the stunning cerulean

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:45.889
waters — but might also catch a glimpse
of some of Prehistorica’s flying pterosaur

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:46.939
species.

00:05:46.939 --> 00:05:51.810
The artist imagines the various pterosaurs
of Prehistorica aren’t contained in an aviary,

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:57.160
but are allowed to nest freely on the coast
thanks to each one being fitted with a tracker.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:01.810
Real world pterosaur fossils have been found
in an incredible number of locations all over

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:05.690
the globe, as this order was extremely diverse
and successful.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:10.370
When first discovered, people in the 18th
and 19th century were baffled by these remains

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:15.210
— and some theorized pterosaurs were actually
a lost order of flying marsupials.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:19.669
Today, we of course know better — although
it’s important to note that despite a common

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:24.759
misconception, pterosaurs are an order separate
from dinosaurs.

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:27.979
On a state-of-the-art ferry, you can tour
Archelon Harbour.

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:32.449
Or, if you’re feeling adventurous, you can
grab yourself a kayak and get even closer

00:06:32.449 --> 00:06:35.569
to some incredible prehistoric life.

00:06:35.569 --> 00:06:40.281
If you paddle near the shallow waters of the
coastal islands, you can watch Archelon Turtles

00:06:40.281 --> 00:06:41.999
glide beneath your boat.

00:06:41.999 --> 00:06:45.820
These enormous marine reptiles might be a
little intimidating due to their sheer size,

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:50.830
but Archelon Turtles are actually some of
the most docile and friendly creatures in

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:51.830
Prehistorica.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:56.630
And I have to say, looking at this scene in
particular… it’s hard not to wish that

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:58.520
this was a real place.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:03.710
Real Archelons weren’t quite as big as Prehistorica’s
resurrections, which the author notes aren’t

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:09.120
always 100% accurate, but they got pretty
close — with some fossils measuring 15 feet,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.680
or 4.5 meters in length.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:17.500
Sea turtles were once thought to be Archelon’s
closest living relatives, but now scientists

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:23.300
theorize Archelon might actually be a completely
separate lineage...

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:27.159
Heading back inland, you can make your way
down to Plesiosaurus Pond.

00:07:27.159 --> 00:07:31.310
Through underground viewing zones, you and
your family can take in the many species from

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:34.059
the Plesiosaurs order that call Prehistorica
their home.

00:07:34.059 --> 00:07:35.059
From behind the safety of the glass, you can
watch Plesiosaurs glide through the water

00:07:35.059 --> 00:07:38.759
and, according to the artist, might even get
a chance to feed them, with a park ranger’s

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:40.469
assistance of course.

00:07:40.469 --> 00:07:44.279
One neat fact to know about the Plesiosaurs
before you toss some food their way is that

00:07:44.279 --> 00:07:48.599
Plesiosaur fossils were discovered as far
back as the 19th century.

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:53.629
One of the most important early specimens
was found by Mary Anning, a pioneering English

00:07:53.629 --> 00:07:58.289
paleontologist who made vital contributions
to the field — though sadly never got the

00:07:58.289 --> 00:08:00.639
credit she deserved in her lifetime.

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:06.010
Recently, however, the plesiosaur genus Anningasaura
was named in her honor!

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:11.259
The sun is now getting low, but we still have
time for a few more stops here at Prehistorica.

00:08:11.259 --> 00:08:15.949
In the fictional Brachylophosaurus Badlands,
if you come at the right time, you could be

00:08:15.949 --> 00:08:21.089
some of the lucky few who get to see the first
interaction between a Brachylophosaurus hatchling

00:08:21.089 --> 00:08:22.720
and the rest of their herd.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:28.190
These beaked dinosaurs are elegant and peaceful,
making these first meetings a beautiful experience

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:30.520
for the guests who get to see one.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:35.400
Millions of years after going extinct, these
remarkable creatures are resuming these ancient

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.210
cycles.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:42.149
Some real Brachylophosaurus specimens are
notable for being exceptionally well-preserved

00:08:42.149 --> 00:08:47.740
— with some remains of these mid-sized hadrosaurids
possessing skin impressions and even the remains

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:51.630
of what they ate recently.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:57.070
It seems their diet included leaves, conifers,
ferns, and flowering plants… and we can

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.610
assume Prehistorica caretakers are feeding
them their favorites.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:06.030
And finally, as the sun sets on the island
of Pangea, we’ll make our last stop on this

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.620
tour millions of years in the making.

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:13.640
The Brachiosaur Redwoods are home to a wide
range of species, the largest of which is

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.380
the titular Brachiosaurus herd.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:21.540
These titanic sauropods occupy a large area,
and migrate each day to a lake where they

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.130
enjoy a swim before moving to the redwoods
to graze.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.810
The artist imagines you can view this vast
herd from treetop walkways, which would be

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:31.640
an incredible sight.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:36.030
While you watch the brachiosaurus munch on
vegetation, you might be interested to learn

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:41.800
these world-famous sauropods have been estimated
at over 66 feet or 20 meters in length — and

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:44.041
might have weighed more than 12 African elephants.

00:09:44.041 --> 00:09:48.120
A seriously impressive genus to end our tour!

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:52.480
Though the sun has set on Prehistorica, you
can check out the work of talented creator

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:56.150
Joshua Dunlop by using the links in the description
below.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:58.070
Also, thanks for watching.

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:02.430
If you enjoyed this entry, you can help make
these videos possible by liking, subscribing,

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:07.080
and hitting the notification icon to stay
up to date on all things Curious.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:20.960
See you in the next video.

